# <file system> <mount point> <type> <options> <dump> <pass>
# The root is the dm-verity device the kernel builds from dm-mod.create= on its command
# line (rootfs.cmdline, written next to the image) — the only device-mapper device there
# is, so dm-0. Not LABEL=root: the filesystem is visible both through the verity device
# and, unverified, on the raw disk underneath it, so the label names two devices.
# EROFS cannot be written to at all, so `ro` is a statement rather than a choice, and
# there is no fsck pass because there is nothing a boot could have left inconsistent.
/dev/dm-0    /       erofs   ro                                0   0

# Everything a running machine writes, in RAM and gone at the next boot. The image ships
# these directories empty; what has to be in them is created at boot by systemd-tmpfiles
# (and by StateDirectory= for units that ask), never copied from the image. /tmp is
# systemd's own tmp.mount and /run is PID 1's, so neither appears here.
#
# /var is not here either, because it is the one that can be persistent. Its tmpfs is
# /usr/lib/systemd/system/var.mount (image/build-rootfs.sh), a fallback rather than a
# rule: a `systemd.mount-extra=<disk>:/var:...` on the kernel command line generates a
# var.mount of its own, which outranks /usr/lib, and the same line here would outrank
# both — and could not be taken back by a VM that has no disk.
tmpfs        /home   tmpfs   mode=0755,nosuid,nodev            0   0
tmpfs        /root   tmpfs   mode=0700,nosuid,nodev            0   0
